				<td width="30%" class="body" valign="top">
					<b><div class="smallbody"><a href="http://www.guildsay.com/user/view_user.php?id=3232">halose7n</a></div></b>
					<div class="xsmall">3/19/2002 - 6:12 PM PST</div>
					<hr width="75%" align="left">
					<div class="xsmall"><a href="http://www.guildsay.com/guilds/view_character.php?id=2882">Serenna Nytefyrre</a></div><div class="xsmall"><a href="http://www.guildsay.com/guilds/view_character.php?id=2588">Laine L`ssinurn</a></div><div class="xsmall"><a href="http://www.guildsay.com/guilds/view_character.php?id=3170">Teriel L`ssinurn</a></div><div class="xsmall"><a href="http://www.guildsay.com/guilds/view_character.php?id=3103">Ellewinne</a></div><div class="xsmall"><a href="http://www.guildsay.com/guilds/view_character.php?id=3214">Rathil</a></div><div class="xsmall"><a href="http://www.guildsay.com/guilds/view_character.php?id=3213">Terial</a></div>					
					<br>
				</td>
				<td width="70%" class="body" valign="top">
					<table width="100%" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0" border="0">
					<tr>
						<td width="100%" class="body" valign="top">
														Everyone has a different opinion of how a guild should be organized and run to be &quot;successful.&quot; I intent to voice my opinion in the document to come, as to hope to shed light upon what my opinion of a successful guild is. By doing this, I hope that everyone will understand my motives.<br />
<br />
Some background about me, regarding guilds and online gaming, in general, is that the first &quot;gaming&quot; I had ever done was roleplaying in chatrooms. From there, I discovered Diablo. I joined a guild, and had TONS of fun with them. Gradually, as the game became older and older, more and more members left (mostly do the way Diablo is played; not updated like EQ is and killing the same mobs over and over for loot that isn't new). From there I played Starcraft a lot, and I do mean a lot. I co-ran a guild for that, which never really got far off the ground due to most of us sucking. It was made up of close friends, and therefore enjoyable, when we weren't trying to beat the crap out of one another. :) Anyway, my point is, both of these quilds were fun because the members were playing together and generally getting along. In Diablo, people of all levels were helping each other out, whether in grouping or helping out with gear, or whatnot. Starcraft was fun because by playing together we were improving our game.<br />
<br />
So, this leads me to the present day. Before I talk about OoN, I would like to comment on Dragons Fist. I came to Rodcet Nife from Bertoxx, because I lost my corpse, and still being a newbie at the time, didn't realize that I could get it back fairly easily. I had taken a few months off because I didn't have time to learn how to play, and when I reactivated my account, my lil' 11 DE SK was in EC, and I ran into something, forgetting that I was KoS and died. I didn't know where I was in EC, so I said screw it. I found a different server to play on for the time being, which was Luclin, to get a fresh start. One day when the servers were down I was hanging out in eq.castersrealm.com's IRC chat and some guy offered to PL me on our server if I made a character. Well, I didn't refuse, and I made it to a big ol' level 3 before he left me to my own devices. He was in Dragons Fist, and a new member himself, who has since left a long time ago (Gellrons if anyone cares to try to remember.) I also met Laldayen around this time, and he helped me get through the low teen levels and he was in DF as well. So, I decided I wanted to join.						</td>
					</tr>
															</table>
				</td>
			</tr>
						<tr>
				<td width="30%" class="body" valign="top">
					<b><div class="smallbody"><a href="http://www.guildsay.com/user/view_user.php?id=3232">halose7n</a></div></b>
					<div class="xsmall">3/19/2002 - 6:20 PM PST</div>
					<hr width="75%" align="left">
					<div class="xsmall"><a href="http://www.guildsay.com/guilds/view_character.php?id=2882">Serenna Nytefyrre</a></div><div class="xsmall"><a href="http://www.guildsay.com/guilds/view_character.php?id=2588">Laine L`ssinurn</a></div><div class="xsmall"><a href="http://www.guildsay.com/guilds/view_character.php?id=3170">Teriel L`ssinurn</a></div><div class="xsmall"><a href="http://www.guildsay.com/guilds/view_character.php?id=3103">Ellewinne</a></div><div class="xsmall"><a href="http://www.guildsay.com/guilds/view_character.php?id=3214">Rathil</a></div><div class="xsmall"><a href="http://www.guildsay.com/guilds/view_character.php?id=3213">Terial</a></div>					
					<br>
				</td>
				<td width="70%" class="body" valign="top">
					<table width="100%" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0" border="0">
					<tr>
						<td width="100%" class="body" valign="top">
														Continuing... (stupid 4096 character limit. :/)<br />
<br />
One of the officers invited me in, though his or her name escapes me. I played Laine a lot then, enjoyed having people to talk to while being bored with downtime or medding in grouping. Everything was fine, until I began to notice how seperated and unorganized the guild really was.<br />
<br />
For one, there is a large level difference between most of the characters in DF. Constantly I would ask if anyone wanted to group, and there wouldn't be a peep out of anyone. Ok, so there's a lack of involvement, no big deal, right? Next, the high level characters in guild, for the most part, tended to associate only with themselves. I won't name names, because it's not my place to cast judgement upon them. They know who they are, whether they realize it or not. The only guild function I ever chose to attend was the raid on the General in Kithikor forest to help Timbernoch attain his epic. I spend 6 hours hanging out with the higher levels in DF, when I was only 24, in an attempt to get to know them. Sadly, Wyztyz and Vdian were the only people who I think appreciated my being there for support. Well, Giek too. Eventually I talked to Aamain a lot, and since then he's someone I can truly call a friend in game, moreso than anyone else. Then, the desparities in DF became worse than better, and I grew tired of dealing with not being able to group with people in the guild to share some comradery, and having my ideas spoken on the guild message board flamed to no end, just because I chose to speak my opinion. Then Salorn was booted unjustly. F1, /guildremove. Period.						</td>
					</tr>
															</table>
				</td>
			</tr>
						<tr>
				<td width="30%" class="body" valign="top">
					<b><div class="smallbody"><a href="http://www.guildsay.com/user/view_user.php?id=3232">halose7n</a></div></b>
					<div class="xsmall">3/19/2002 - 6:27 PM PST</div>
					<hr width="75%" align="left">
					<div class="xsmall"><a href="http://www.guildsay.com/guilds/view_character.php?id=2882">Serenna Nytefyrre</a></div><div class="xsmall"><a href="http://www.guildsay.com/guilds/view_character.php?id=2588">Laine L`ssinurn</a></div><div class="xsmall"><a href="http://www.guildsay.com/guilds/view_character.php?id=3170">Teriel L`ssinurn</a></div><div class="xsmall"><a href="http://www.guildsay.com/guilds/view_character.php?id=3103">Ellewinne</a></div><div class="xsmall"><a href="http://www.guildsay.com/guilds/view_character.php?id=3214">Rathil</a></div><div class="xsmall"><a href="http://www.guildsay.com/guilds/view_character.php?id=3213">Terial</a></div>					
					<br>
				</td>
				<td width="70%" class="body" valign="top">
					<table width="100%" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0" border="0">
					<tr>
						<td width="100%" class="body" valign="top">
														Continued, x2. :)<br />
<br />
Dragons Fist is going nowhere fast, as much as they think they are. They will never be an Avatars of Discord, or an Afterlife, or any other &quot;uber guild,&quot; which so many of the 50+ officers are trying to make them into be (one comes to mind right away..). They violate their charter to the core, as well as their rules, and to an extent I am ashamed that we share the same charter. I do not mean for anyone in DF who may be reading this to take offense, but it is MY opinion, for whatever it's worth.<br />
<br />
So, when Aamain said that he was making his own guild, I jumped on the idea. I think with all the posting on the DF message board and talking to him that I did, that I guess I proved my knowledge and value. That is what I wish to bring to the Order of Nobleshadows, and I think that's what many of the officers wish to do as well.<br />
<br />
Success for our guild to me is communication, as Aamain states ALL THE TIME. It's true. Without communication, the guild will stagnate from a lack of new ideas and perspective upon what is happening in &quot;our world&quot; of Norrath. We may never become an uberguild, which I would be very pleased to see if we did, but damn, we can try, can't we?<br />
<br />
First of all, leveling is the single most important thing we can do, until everyone has a main character AT LEAST 50. Yes yes, I'm one to talk, but it's true. From there, we can do all the fun things like raiding, high level questing, etc, and prove how much knowledge we've acrued in playing the game. Grouping together is one way to achieve this goal, and it is twofold. Not only do we gain experience from a character perspective, but we grow as a guild and can trust in each other by learning how we play together.<br />
<br />
So, that's the point of my drivel. Let's level, let's have fun, and let's do it together.<br />
<br />
Serenna, 29 monk LFG.						</td>
					</tr>
